    "text": "Naming Amines"
  },
  "documentClass": null,
  "metadata": {
    "uuid": "791e44dd-7a70-4db5-b07d-84447678697d"
  },
  "content": [
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00001",
      "segmentId": "m00289:para:para-00001"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00001",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00289:alt:fig-00001-alt",
          "text": "Three structures show tertiary-butyl alcohol (a tertiary alcohol), trimethylamine (a tertiary amine) and tertiary butyl amine (a primary amine)."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_24_01_001.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00002",
      "segmentId": "m00289:para:para-00002"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00002",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00289:alt:fig-00002-alt",
          "text": "The structure of a quaternary ammonium salt, which is a nitrogen positive linked to four R groups and an X negative ion."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_24_01_002.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00003",
      "segmentId": "m00289:para:para-00003"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00003",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00289:alt:fig-00003-alt",
          "text": "The structures of tertiary-butyl amine, cyclohexylamine, and aniline. Aniline is enclosed in square parentheses."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_24_01_003.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00004",
      "segmentId": "m00289:para:para-00004"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00004",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00289:alt:fig-00004-alt",
          "text": "The structures of 4,4-dimethylcyclohexanamine and 1,4-butanediamine. 4,4-dimethylcyclohexanamine has cyclohexane ring with two methyl groups on C4 and amine on C1. 1,4-butanediamine is four-carbon chain with amines on C1 and C4."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_24_01_004.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00005",
      "segmentId": "m00289:para:para-00005"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00005",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00289:alt:fig-00005-alt",
          "text": "The structures of 2-aminobutanoic acid, 2,4-diaminobenzoic acid and 4-amino-2-butanone. The carbon atoms of 2-aminobutanoic acid and 4-amino-2-butanone are numbered explicitly."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_24_01_005.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00006",
      "segmentId": "m00289:para:para-00006"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00006",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00289:alt:fig-00006-alt",
          "text": "The structures of diphenylamine and triethylamine. Diphenylamine has two benzene rings linked to an N H group. Triethylamine has a nitrogen atom linked to three ethyl groups."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_24_01_006.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00007",
      "segmentId": "m00289:para:para-00007"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00007",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00289:alt:fig-00007-alt",
          "text": "N,N-dimethylpropylamine has a nitrogen linked to two methyl and a propyl group. N-ethyl-N-methylcyclohexylamine has cyclohexane linked to a nitrogen. This is connected to methyl and ethyl group."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_24_01_007.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00008",
      "segmentId": "m00289:para:para-00008"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00008",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00289:alt:fig-00008-alt",
          "text": "The structures of pyridine, pyrrole, quinoline, imidazole, indole, pyrimidine, pyrrolidine, and piperidine. The compounds are numbered from the nitrogen atom."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_24_01_008.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
